    {
      "id": "tn12m-mat-09",
      "chapter": "Applications of Matrices and Determinants",
      "topic": "Matrix inversion method",
      "type": "long",
      "marks": 5,
      "q": "Solve by the matrix inversion method: $x+y+z=6$, $2x-y+z=3$, $x+2y-z=2$.",
      "solution": "Write $AX=B$ with $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1&1&1\\\\2&-1&1\\\\1&2&-1\\end{bmatrix}$, $B=\\begin{bmatrix}6\\\\3\\\\2\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\n$|A|=1(1-2)-1(-2-1)+1(4+1)=-1+3+5=7\\ne0$, so $A^{-1}$ exists.\n\nCofactors give $\\operatorname{adj}A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1&3&2\\\\3&-2&1\\\\5&-1&-3\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\n$X=A^{-1}B=\\dfrac17\\begin{bmatrix}-1&3&2\\\\3&-2&1\\\\5&-1&-3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6\\\\3\\\\2\\end{bmatrix}=\\dfrac17\\begin{bmatrix}7\\\\14\\\\21\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\2\\\\3\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nSo $x=1$, $y=2$, $z=3$. Check: $2-2+3=3$ and $1+4-3=2$."
